Call <code>bucket.put(c, key, .{ .bytes = …, .content_type = … })</code> also reads <code>.cache_control</code> and <code>.content_disposition</code> if the value has them <code>bucket.putStream(c, key, .{ .reader = …, .len = …, .content_type = … })</code> from a <code>*std.Io.Reader</code> of a known length <code>bucket.putMultipart(c, key, .{ .reader = …, .content_type = … })</code> from a <code>*std.Io.Reader</code> of unknown length: S3's multipart upload, the whole protocol in one call. Below <code>bucket.delete(c, key)</code> <p><code>put</code> takes any value with <code>.bytes</code> and <code>.content_type</code>, checked while compiling. A <code>nilo.Upload</code> already has that shape, so a file from a form goes straight through:</p> <pre><code>const NewAvatar = struct { image: nilo.Upload };

fn setAvatar(avatars: *Avatars, c: *nilo.Ctx, account: u32, incoming: nilo.Form(NewAvatar)) !nilo.Status(201, void) {
    var key: [32]u8 = undefined;
    try avatars.put(c, try std.fmt.bufPrint(&amp;key, "{d}.png", .{account}), incoming.value.image);
    return .{};
}
</code></pre> <p>Choose the key yourself, and do not use <code>image.filename</code> for it: that is whatever the browser sent, <code>../../etc/passwd</code> included. <code>nilo_s3</code> encodes the key into a URL, once. Deciding what an object is called is the application's job.</p> <p><code>putStream</code> sends a length, never a chunked body, because S3 answers <code>411</code> to a body whose length it was not told. The length is a required field rather than an optional one, so "I do not know the length" is a compile error here instead of an S3 status code. An upload whose size is unknown before it starts is <code>putMultipart</code>'s.</p>
